;;; A query's claim about the int `1` held of a state holding the name `"1"`
;;; — **W28**, regression `claim-int-name`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds "1")`, a string written as a fact's argument, and a
;;; query whose `:expect` claims the one class `(model (holds 1))`. The claim
;;; is the witness: it is false, and it stays written as it is.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string is natural-language text, never a value (Q-M1e2.23), so the
;;; program is refused at the parse, at `"1"`: every command that reads it
;;; exits 1 (`ein test`: 2). A run of digits is a name (Q-M1e2.24), so the
;;; case this file was written for, a string and a name or an int of one
;;; text, can no longer be written; the file stays as the refusal's witness.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `86227f9a` the loader held a string argument as the name it spells, a
;;; value apart from the int `1`, and a claim was compared with the state as
;;; printed text. The printer printed a name as its raw text, so the int `1`
;;; and the name `"1"` were one text, `(holds 1)`: R was root's state, the
;;; claim held, and `ein test -v FILE` said `holds` and exited 0, in a release
;;; build and a debug one.
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation holds T)
(holds "1")
(query :goal (holds ?v) :expect (model (holds 1)))
